From: Gezim <gezimos@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kompakt: the front light's PWM by a square law over a 12-bit
 period, so dim light has steps

Android sends the front light 255 steps. Stock mapped them linearly to 127
duties (CONFIG_LIGHTNESS_MAPPING_VALUE) and those to the 10-bit PWM in steps
of 4, capped at half the range. A front light on e-ink lives in the bottom
few percent of that: the 2 to 4 percent people run it at were duties 2 to 5,
five distinct levels, each 25 to 100 percent more light than the one before,
so every change of room the auto curve followed was a visible jump, however
slowly the framework ramped.

The front light (led_mode 5, BLS PWM) takes its PWM straight from the level
by a square law. The other LED modes keep the stock mapping; everything else
that reads the backlight gets level_1024, 510 * (level / 255)^2 of 1023, never
below 1 while on, the same top as stock's 510.

DISP_PWM's CON_1 period field is 12 bits on this IP generation (the same
register layout as the 12-bit parts the upstream pwm-mtk-disp driver
handles). The first time the front light is set, the driver writes a 4095
period and reads it back. If it took, the clock divider drops by the same
factor, (div + 1) * (period + 1) unchanged (div 3 at 1023 becomes div 0 at
4095), so the PWM frequency is exactly what it was, and every later config
keeps the pair. The front light then sends a fine duty, 2040 * (level / 255)^2
of 4096: the same light at every level with sixteen times stock's steps at
the dim end, twenty distinct values over Android levels 2 to 28. If the period
did not take, the old period is put back and the light runs on level_1024. A
one-time kernel line says which; a re-apply through the stock path (thermal
limits) reuses the last fine duty. The backlight_debug_log "map:" is the fine
duty, 0 to 2040.

The Kompakt app's curve (LightCurve.kt) and the overlay curve carry their
levels in these steps; the app rewrites a curve saved in the old steps once
at boot.
